{"id": "q0387", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "aggressive", "tier": "aggressive", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 4286911674, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "c1188d4ab398808355719c420f025bd920b27aba798f5596eb328457a751cc63", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.5533501505851746, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 1, "query": "Traditionally with what were the seams of wooden ships caulked?", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0387.wav", "input_sha256": "5a89a80b69f1780a8f287866578a885d6d574f306f5c438aa1cf2aa3619e0648", "input_samples": 62800, "input_s": 3.925, "n_real_chunks": 4, "score": 0.3086, "act": 0.9998, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Traditionally, the seam s of wooden ships were caulked with a mixture called pitch and oakum. P itch is derived from wood or coal, and it acts as an adhesive to seal gaps. Oakum, which is made from twisted strands of old rope fibers, was packed into those gaps and then covered with more pitch. This combination helped prevent water from seeping through the seams, keeping the ship dry and seaworthy. The process required skilled work men known as caulk ers who carefully applied the materials by hand, often using tools like a caulking iron to spread and smooth the pitch.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 4, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 28, "feed_lag_max_ms": 20, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 20, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0387_aggressive_local.wav", "sha256": "b50a9eaa3991025ade45349eb3ee6f80a844b87a7cc15f250372709cfdeb4741", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 28.28}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.081, 1.147, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.085, 2.154, 1, 0,